## Why This Is Asked

Streaming APIs combined with REST surfaces are common in large-scale systems. This question probes testing of delivery guarantees, ordering, resilience under partial outages, and observability—areas critical for Snap, Microsoft, and Google-scale services.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing across REST and WebSocket channels
- Message ordering and sequencing guarantees
- Delivery semantics (at-least-once vs exactly-once) and replay safety
- Reconnection, backoff, and backfill strategies using durable offsets
- Handling partial upstream outages and dynamic schema changes
- Observability with tracing, logs, and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic WebSocket stream ordering test (conceptual)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/stream');
let lastSeq = -1;
let ok = true;
let count = 0;
ws.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data);
  if (typeof msg.seq !== 'number' || msg.seq <= lastSeq) ok = false;
  lastSeq = msg.seq;
  count++;
  if (count >= 100) ws.close();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate network partitions and validate reconnect/backoff behavior across regions?
- How would you validate exactly-once semantics when an event is replayed during backfill?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical API versioning and deprecation testing strategies, assessing the candidate's ability to design comprehensive test coverage for versioned APIs with concrete expectations and appropriate tooling recommendations.

## Key Concepts

- Version routing via HTTP headers
- Backward compatibility validation between API versions
- Deprecation signaling and migration path implementation
- Negative testing and edge case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
describe('GET /users/{id} versioned endpoints', () => {
  it('defaults to v1 when Version header is missing', async () => {
    const res = await request.get('/users/123')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('username')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('email')
    expect(res.body).not.toHaveProperty('phone')
  })

  it('returns v2 format with include_email=true', async () => {
    const res = await request
      .get('/users/123?include_email=true')
      .set('X-API-Version', 'v2')
    expect(res.body).toEqual({
      username: expect.any(String),
      email: expect.any(String),
      phone: expect.any(String),
      address: expect.any(Object)
    })
  })
})

